  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying and comparing the most probable outcomes (i.e., the outcome with the highest probability in each lottery). In this case, both A and B have 2400 Euros as their most probable outcome (with 100% and 66% respectively), so the reason would not favor one over the other. The individual, however, focuses on the certainty of A versus the risk in B and the small improvement in the best-case scenario of B, which reflects a certainty-based or risk-aversion argument, not a comparison of most probable outcomes. There is no indication that the individual isolated the 'most probable outcome' as a concept or used it in their comparison. Therefore, while the outcome 2400 Euros is mentioned implicitly, it is not framed in terms of probability dominance. The justification is more aligned with a certainty effect or risk aversion than with the specific rule of the decision reason. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

